"[2510.11512v1] LikePhys: Evaluating Intuitive Physics Understanding in Video Diffusion Models via Likelihood Preference (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11512v1)\nciteturn2831view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11512v1\",\"lineno\":15}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 6 Mar 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:LikePhys: Evaluating Intuitive Physics Understanding in Video Diffusion Models via Likelihood Preference\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Jianhao Yuan , cite7†Fabio Pizzati , cite8†Francesco Pinto , cite9†Lars Kunze , cite10†Ivan Laptev , cite11†Paul Newman , cite12†Philip Torr , cite13†Daniele De Martini L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled LikePhys: Evaluating Intuitive Physics Understanding in Video Diffusion Models via Likelihood Preference, by Jianhao Yuan and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Intuitive physics understanding in video diffusion models plays an essential role in building general-purpose physically plausible world simulators, yet accurately evaluating such capacity remains a challenging task due to the difficulty in disentangling physics correctness from visual appearance in generation.\nL17: To the end, we introduce LikePhys, a training-free method that evaluates intuitive physics in video diffusion models by distinguishing physically valid and impossible videos using the denoising objective as an ELBO-based likelihood surrogate on a curated dataset of valid-invalid pairs.\nL18: By testing on our constructed benchmark of twelve scenarios spanning over four physics domains, we show that our evaluation metric, Plausibility Preference Error (PPE), demonstrates strong alignment with human preference, outperforming state-of-the-art evaluator baselines. We then systematically benchmark intuitive physics understanding in current video diffusion models.\nL19: Our study further analyses how model design and inference settings affect intuitive physics understanding and highlights domain-specific capacity variations across physical laws. Empirical results show that, despite current models struggling with complex and chaotic dynamics, there is a clear trend of improvement in physics understanding as model capacity and inference settings scale.\nL20: Comments:  | 22 pages, 9 figures\nL21: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2510.11512 [cs.CV]\nL23:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2510.11512v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL24:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11512†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jianhao Yuan [cite19†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 15:19:07 UTC (28,443 KB)\nL29: cite20†[v2] Tue, 25 Nov 2025 14:24:21 UTC (4,015 KB)\nL30: cite5†[v3] Fri, 6 Mar 2026 07:36:35 UTC (4,016 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled LikePhys: Evaluating Intuitive Physics Understanding in Video Diffusion Models via Likelihood Preference, by Jianhao Yuan and 7 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite14†View PDF L38:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite21†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite22†view license†creativecommons.org L42: \nL43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.CV\nL46: \nL47: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L48: \nL49: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2025-10 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite28†cs L54: cite29†c