racking with Video Diffusion Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ayush Shrivastava , cite7†Sanyam Mehta , cite8†Daniel Geng , cite9†Andrew Owens L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Point Prompting: Counterfactual Tracking with Video Diffusion Models, by Ayush Shrivastava and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Trackers and video generators solve closely related problems: the former analyze motion, while the latter synthesize it. We show that this connection enables pretrained video diffusion models to perform zero-shot point tracking by simply prompting them to visually mark points as they move over time. We place a distinctively colored marker at the query point, then regenerate the rest of the video from an intermediate noise level.\nL17: This propagates the marker across frames, tracing the point's trajectory. To ensure that the marker remains visible in this counterfactual generation, despite such markers being unlikely in natural videos, we use the unedited initial frame as a negative prompt.\nL18: Through experiments with multiple image-conditioned video diffusion models, we find that these \"emergent\" tracks outperform those of prior zero-shot methods and persist through occlusions, often obtaining performance that is competitive with specialized self-supervised models.\nL19: Comments:  | Project link: cite12†this https URL†point-prompting.github.io L20: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2510.11715 [cs.CV]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2510.11715v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL23:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11715†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Ayush Shrivastava [cite16†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 17:59:46 UTC (7,353 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Tue, 14 Apr 2026 17:57:46 UTC (7,364 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Acce